@page "/tables/simple-table/filter/interaction"

@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button.Enums
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.Shared.Models
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Components
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Components.Columns
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Components.Filters
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Models

<h3>Filter interaction</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The table owns the filter it applies — no parent holds it, so no parent re-render can disturb it. These are the four
    channels that move it: a starting value it adopts once, an observation of every change, a two-way binding, and a
    call that commands a change from outside. Every table below uses the pre-made contains filter, so nothing here
    depends on writing a filter of your own.
</DescriptionBanner>

<h3>Start with a filter — an unchanging <code>FilterState</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Pass <code>FilterState</code> from a field that is never reassigned to give the table the filter it starts
    with. <code>SimpleTable</code> forwards it to the wrapped <code>AdvancedTable</code>, which applies it once
    the columns have registered and owns it from then on, because handing over the same instance again is not a
    new command. The filter this table starts with:
</DescriptionBanner>

<ul>
    @foreach (var activeFilter in _seedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
    {
        <li>
            <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
            @if (activeFilter is SimpleTableContainsColumnFilter<ExampleTableItem> containsFilter)
            {
                <text> — contains "@containsFilter.Value"</text>
            }
        </li>
    }
</ul>

<SimpleTable Items="s_items" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" FilterState="_seedFilterState" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Key" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Value" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Observe changes — <code>FilterStateChanged</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Handle <code>FilterStateChanged</code> without passing <code>FilterState</code> to watch the applied filter
    without driving it. It reports the applied filter after every change, whatever caused it. The list below
    reports what was last raised.
</DescriptionBanner>

@if (_observedFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _observedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is SimpleTableContainsColumnFilter<ExampleTableItem> containsFilter)
                {
                    <text> — contains "@containsFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>Nothing observed yet — open a column filter and apply.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable Items="s_items" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" FilterStateChanged="ObservedFilterStateChanged" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Key" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Value" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Drive the filter from markup — <code>@("@bind-FilterState")</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Bind <code>FilterState</code> to drive the table from a field and have every change written back into it,
    whoever made it — the buttons below or a column header. A new instance in the field is a command; the same
    instance again is not, which is what lets the table's own value flow back without starting over.

    <strong>Note:</strong> build the value in a field and only replace it when it really changes. A parent that
    produces a fresh, content-equal instance on every render makes the table re-apply on every render, and under
    a binding that never terminates — each apply reports back, each report renders the parent, each render
    builds another instance.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Key filter</label>
        <Button Text="Keys containing b" OnClick="FilterBoundTableByKey" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
    <div class="option">
        <label>All column filters</label>
        <Button Text="Clear filters" OnClick="ClearBoundFilter" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_boundFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _boundFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is SimpleTableContainsColumnFilter<ExampleTableItem> containsFilter)
                {
                    <text> — contains "@containsFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active filter.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable Items="s_items"
             RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
             @bind-FilterState="_boundFilterState"
             MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Key" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Value" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Change the filter from outside — <code>SetFilterStateAsync</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Hold the table in an <code>@("@ref")</code> and call <code>SetFilterStateAsync</code> for the changes a binding
    expresses badly — a "clear all" button, a reset to a preset — and for callers that are not in a render at all.
    Unlike the parameter the method has no memory of what it was handed before, so clearing works here even though the
    empty filter is a value the table has seen before.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>All column filters</label>
        <Button Text="Clear filters" OnClick="ClearFiltersAsync" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_pushedFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _pushedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is SimpleTableContainsColumnFilter<ExampleTableItem> containsFilter)
                {
                    <text> — contains "@containsFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active filter.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable @ref="_pushTargetTable"
             Items="s_items"
             RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
             FilterStateChanged="PushedFilterStateChanged"
             MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Key" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <SimpleTableContainsColumnFilterEditor ValueSelector="x => x.Value" />
            </FilterEditor>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>
